<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        EVERY STYLE IN THIS WINDOW WHOSE TARGET IS A TextBlock, AND NOTHING ELSE. Split out of
        Themes/Controls.xaml on 2026-08-25, unchanged and in the order they were in.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other theme files: not one value of its own, every number
        and colour below is a name declared in Values.xaml.

        IT WENT BECAUSE Controls.xaml STOOD TWO LINES UNDER THE MARKUP CEILING - 395 against 397,
        measured 2026-08-19 - and backlog 213 named this seam six days before it was needed. The
        thing that asked for it is a third style in the column picker, and the packet after that
        one puts a bar of buttons over the list, which is more control styles again. A file with
        two lines of room is a file where the next change starts with looking for a seam, so the
        seam was worth taking before the change rather than during it.

        THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT AND NOT A LINE COUNT, which is the rule every other split in this
        window followed. What is here is TEXT - how a sentence, a heading, a step and a count are
        drawn. What stayed is the CONTROLS - the field somebody types into, the box somebody
        ticks, the chip, the menu item.

        WHERE IT IS MERGED IS LOAD BEARING AND IT IS NOT THE END OF THE LIST. Controls.xaml
        itself reaches SubduedText through StaticResource, which resolves WHILE THAT FILE IS
        BEING READ, so this dictionary goes BEFORE it - after the values it reads and before the
        styles that read it. App.xaml carries that order, and so do the three other places that
        build this stack: WpfHost in the tests, tools/wpfui-probe and tools/gui-probe/check.ps1.

        NEITHER THIS FILE NOR Controls.xaml LOADS ON ITS OWN. Both reach WPF UI's keys and both
        reach ours in Values.xaml. The implicit TextBlock style below is based BY NAME on the
        control library's own, for the reason its comment gives.
    -->
    <!--
        BasedOn by NAME on WPF UI's own styles, not on {x:Type TextBlock}.

        The type form worked while the base was the built-in Fluent theme, and would now be
        ambiguous: this dictionary is merged after theirs and is itself keyed by the same type,
        so which style it resolves to depends on lookup order rather than on intent. Their keys
        are stable, documented by their own markup - DataGrid.xaml bases four styles on exactly
        these names - and they say what is meant.

        CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW: this file no longer loads on its own. It resolves keys that only
        exist once ControlsDictionary is merged, which is why tools/wpfui-probe loads it after
        theirs. That is a real dependency and it is the shape of the decision the owner made.

        Every one of the three below keeps OUR size setter on top of their template. That is
        the density decision of 2026-08-02 in the two places it is spelled out.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A PANEL'S OWN TITLE. SemiBold since 2026-08-19, and measured rather than preferred: it set a
        size and no weight, so it arrived Normal while the sentence under it was SemiBold - the
        largest text on the panel was the THINNEST thing on it. Both panels using this key had the
        same fault, so one setter fixes both.
    -->
    <!--
        THE COLOUR IS NAMED HERE, AND UNTIL 2026-09-01 IT WAS NOT. A text style that sets no
        Foreground does not have a default colour - it has whatever contains it, and that is a
        different answer in every place the style is used.
        <b>What it cost, measured rather than argued.</b> The machine overview draws each number
        inside a Button whose style carries no BasedOn, so the button never picked up the theme's
        Foreground and fell back to the system default, which is BLACK. Against the window at
        #202020 that is a contrast ratio of 1.29 - `tools/contrast/contrast.ps1`, and WCAG asks 3.0
        of large text. The headline numbers of the first screen anybody sees were invisible, and
        the qualification lines under them were readable, because those have a trigger that names
        a colour. The owner found it by looking.
        <b>Why the repair is here and not on the button:</b> the button was one place this style is
        used and the fault is that the style had no answer of its own. Naming the colour fixes
        every present and future container at once, and `AppearanceGuards` now refuses a TextBlock
        style in this theme that does not name one.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="HeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The same repair, and this one had not gone wrong yet. It is used for the command a person
        can copy out of the plan panel, which sits on a surface that happened to hand down a
        readable colour - a fault waiting for the first container that does not.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="FixedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubduedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A HEADING INSIDE A PANEL, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME THING AS THE PANEL'S OWN TITLE. Added
        2026-08-19, after the owner looked at the plan panel and could not find where to start
        reading: five headings and the title were all TextSizeHeading, so nothing was the top of
        anything.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeNormal because 14 is the floor for semibold text in
        the guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and a section heading in a wall of sentences has to
        be seen without being read.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SectionHeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHERE THE PLAN PANEL IS IN ITS SEQUENCE - nothing done, doing it, done.
        <b>NOT NoticeText, and that was the fault the owner saw.</b> This line wore the warning
        orange, which is the same colour as the line at the bottom of the window saying the
        session has no administrator rights - so the sentence telling somebody that NOTHING HAS
        HAPPENED read as something having gone wrong.

        <b>SEMIBOLD CAME OFF ON 2026-08-19, THE DAY AFTER IT WENT ON, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY.</b>
        This style and SectionHeadingText above had IDENTICAL setters - same face, same 14, same
        SemiBold, same colour - so "Nothing has been done" was rendered exactly like "In this
        order:". The panel had four headings and no body. Fixing the colour fault above created a
        weight fault here, which is the shape rule 11 of the project notes is about: a correction is
        a claim and needs checking as one.
        Size still separates it from the body at 13 and position separates it from everything -
        it is the only line between the title and the sections. Weight is what it gives up, because
        weight is now the only thing that says "this is a section label".
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanNoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE STEP OF A PLAN, AND UNTIL 2026-08-19 IT HAD NO STYLE OF ITS OWN. It was built on
        SubduedText, which is TextSizeSmall - so what will happen to the machine was rendered at 12
        under a section heading at 14 SemiBold. The label was larger and heavier than the thing it
        labelled, and the eye slid off three bold labels without stopping on any content.
        The subdued FOREGROUND is kept and the subdued SIZE is not: a step nobody asked for should be
        dimmer rather than smaller, because `C2` wants those findable at a glance and a size
        difference makes them hard to read rather than easy to skip.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanStepText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE COUNT, WHICH MOVED OUT FROM UNDER THE LIST AND STOPPED BEING SUBDUED. Complaint 8 of
        the eleven says it was the least visible thing on the screen and is the main answer to
        every keystroke - small, grey and at the bottom is three ways of saying "ignore me".

        SEMIBOLD WHEN THE LIST IS NARROWED, AND THAT IS COMPLAINT 7 RATHER THAN DECORATION. That
        one says you cannot see THAT a filter is on. The words already say it - "108 of 810"
        against "810" - but a weight change is seen without reading, which is the difference
        between a signal and a fact you have to go and check.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeHeading: 14 is the floor for semibold text in the
        guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and this line is semibold half the time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CountLine" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />

        <!--
            Missing until 2026-08-10, and the count was black on a dark window. A regression from
            slice 5, where it "stopped being grey" - the brush was removed rather than replaced,
            so this style declared type and no colour and inherited one nobody chose. Backlog 145.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Narrowed}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The sentence in the middle of an empty list. Larger than the rows, because it is the only
        thing on that half of the screen and somebody is looking at it wondering what happened.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="EmptyStateText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        What the result has to admit about itself, and what is wrong with the question. Two
        styles rather than one, because they answer different questions and a person glancing
        at the window has to be able to tell "your query is broken" from "your query worked and
        the answer has holes in it" without reading either sentence.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="NoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ProblemText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE FOUR VOICES OF THE MACHINE OVERVIEW - `G`, rebuilt as cards on 2026-09-01 after the
        owner rejected the line-by-line version three times. The argument for the shape is beside
        PadTile in Values.xaml. What belongs here is why there are FOUR of them and not two.

        THE SCREEN HAS TWO KINDS OF THING ON IT AND EACH OF THEM IS A PAIR. A finding is a number
        and what it counts. A qualification is a smaller number and what IT counts. Drawing either
        pair in one style is what the earlier versions did, and it is why the screen read as six
        equal lines when it is three findings, two of which have something to add.

        AND THE LABELS ARE FULL STRENGTH, WHICH IS THE HALF OF THE OWNER'S COMPLAINT THAT WENT
        UNANSWERED TWICE. "Dark texts, you cannot see anything" was two faults wearing one sentence.
        The first was measured and repaired - HeadingText named no colour, so the numbers fell to
        system black at a contrast of 1.29. The second was never touched: the labels beside them
        were TextSubdued, so REPAIRING the first fault left a screen that was still mostly grey, and
        the version after it added more dimming as a design and made the complaint worse. Subdued
        survives here in exactly one place - a qualification, which genuinely is subordinate to the
        number over it - and it is now three lines of the screen rather than most of it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="OverviewNumberText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeDisplay}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="OverviewLabelText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        SemiBold at 14 rather than at 13, and the floor is why. Microsoft's guidance puts plain text
        at 12 and semibold at 14, because a heavier stroke at a small size fills its own counters in
        - TypeScaleGuards holds both numbers, so a qualification's number set semibold at
        TextSizeNormal would fail the build rather than merely look worse.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="OverviewQualificationNumberText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="OverviewQualificationText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
